.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
form
- format text
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
form [ -t ] [ -o file ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Form reads text, fills and formats it, and writes it out paginated.
With no file it reads the standard input.
.sp
A line whose first character is a period is a request.  Everything
else is text.  In fill mode, which is the default, words are gathered
onto a line until the next one would pass the right margin.  A break
ends the line being filled and starts a new one; most requests that
change the shape of the page take one first.  In no-fill mode a line
of input is a line of output.
.sp
The page is sixty-six lines long with a header three lines down and a
footer three lines from the bottom, both set by request.  A page fills
to within six lines of the bottom and then breaks.
.sp
Form is the formatter the manual is written in.  There are no macros:
a page calls these requests directly.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-t	For a terminal: do not write the long runs of blank lines that separate one page from the next, so that the text does not scroll away.

.ti -10
-o	Write to the named file rather than to the standard output.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
REQUESTS
.in +5
.br
An argument is a number, which may be signed.  Where a request takes
one and none is given, the default below is used.
.sp
.nf
     .bd n   embolden the next n lines   (1)
     .bp n   begin a page, numbered n    (the next)
     .br     break                       
     .ce n   centre the next n lines     (1)
     .fi     fill                        (the default)
     .fo T   footer text, in quotes      
     .he T   header text, in quotes      
     .in n   indent n columns            (0)
     .ls n   line spacing                (1)
     .nf     no fill                     
     .pl n   page length                 (66)
     .rm n   right margin                (60)
     .sp n   space n lines               (1)
     .ta n   tab stop                    (8)
     .ti n   indent the next line only   (0)
     .ul n   underline the next n lines  (1)
     ...     insert a literal .
.fi
.sp
An indent written +n or -n is relative to the one in force; written
plain it is absolute, and with no argument at all it returns to the
left margin.  An indent never goes negative.
.sp
A count applies to lines of output rather than of input, so a request
to embolden filled text emboldens the line the words land on and not
the line they were typed on.  This is why a word emboldened in running
prose sits on a line of its own.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A request form does not know makes its line vanish rather than
producing a diagnostic, so prose that begins with a period is lost.
To begin a line of text with a literal period, write three dots: ...
emits one dot and then the rest of the line as text.  Several pages
of the original manual are short a line for want of this.
.sp
The text of the header and the footer is taken in quotes and used
literally, except that a number sign in it is replaced by the page
number.  Every page in this manual carries its page number that way.
.sp
The tests of the page length and the line spacing are thin: the
original manual documents both requests and uses neither.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
